<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-laplace-ivp-example" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Solving an IVP with the Laplace Transform</title>

  <p>
    We have finally established all the theory needed regarding Laplace transforms, and we are ready to use it to solve an 
    ODE!
    Recall the two key formulas from <xref ref="thm-laplace-derivatives"/>:
    <md>
      <mrow>\mathcal{L}\{x'(t)\} \amp= sX(s) - x(0)</mrow>
      <mrow>\mathcal{L}\{x''(t)\} \amp= s^2 X(s) - sx(0) - x'(0).</mrow>
    </md>
    Also note that <m>\mathcal{L}\{0\} = 0</m>, since the transform of the zero function is zero,
    and <m>\mathcal{L}\{x\} = X(s)</m> by definition.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-laplace-ivp-first">
    <title>Solving an IVP Using the Laplace Transform</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Solve the initial value problem
        <me>x'' - x' - 6x = 0, \quad x(0) = 2,\ x'(0) = -1.</me>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ── TASK A: SUBSTITUTE ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-ivp-substitute">
      <title>Step 1a: Substitute the Derivative Formulas</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Apply <m>\mathcal{L}</m> to both sides of the ODE and use
          <xref ref="thm-laplace-derivatives"/> to replace <m>x''</m>, <m>x'</m>, and <m>x</m>
          with expressions in <m>X(s)</m>, plugging in <m>x(0) = 2</m> and <m>x'(0) = -1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Apply <m>\mathcal{L}</m> to each term separately using linearity, then substitute
          <m>\mathcal{L}\{x''\} = s^2X(s) - sx(0) - x'(0)</m>,
          <m>\mathcal{L}\{x'\} = sX(s) - x(0)</m>, and
          <m>\mathcal{L}\{x\} = X(s)</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Applying <m>\mathcal{L}</m> to both sides and using linearity:
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathcal{L}\{x''-x' - 6x\} \amp= \mathcal{L}\{0\}.</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathcal{L}\{x''\} - \mathcal{L}\{x'\} - 6\,\mathcal{L}\{x\} \amp= 0.</mrow>
          </md>
          Substituting the derivative formulas with <m>x(0) = 2</m> and <m>x'(0) = -1</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>\underbrace{\bigl(s^2 X(s) - 2s + 1\bigr)}_{\mathcal{L}\{x''\}}
              - \underbrace{\bigl(sX(s) - 2\bigr)}_{\mathcal{L}\{x'\}}
              - 6\underbrace{X(s)}_{\mathcal{L}\{x\}} \amp= 0.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- ── TASK B: SOLVE FOR X(s) ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-ivp1-solve">
      <title>Step 1b: Solve for <m>X(s)</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Starting from
          <me>\bigl(s^2 X(s) - 2s + 1\bigr) - \bigl(sX(s) - 2\bigr) - 6X(s) = 0,</me>
          collect all <m>X(s)</m> terms on the left and everything else on the right,
          then solve for <m>X(s)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Expand, group all terms with <m>X(s)</m> on the left and move all other terms
          to the right, then factor out <m>X(s)</m> and divide.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Expanding and collecting:
          <md>
            <mrow>s^2 X(s) - 2s + 1 - sX(s) + 2 - 6X(s) \amp= 0</mrow>
            <mrow>(s^2 - s - 6)\,X(s) \amp= 2s - 3.</mrow>
          </md>
          Solving for <m>X(s)</m>:
          <me>X(s) = \frac{2s - 3}{s^2 - s - 6}.</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- ── TASK C: PFD ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-ivp-pfd">
      <title>Step 2: Partial Fraction Decomposition</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor the denominator: <m>s^2 - s - 6 = (s-3)(s+2)</m>.
          This is the same type as in <xref ref="ex-pf-clear-denominators"/>, two distinct linear factors.
          We will skip the details on the computation. The partial fraction decomposition gives:
          <me>X(s) = \frac{2s-3}{(s-3)(s+2)} = \frac{\frac{3}{5}}{s-3} + \frac{\frac{7}{5}}{s+2}.</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <!-- ── TASK D: INVERSE LAPLACE ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-ivp1-inverse">
      <title>Step 3: Apply the Inverse Laplace Transform</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Apply <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}</m> to <m>X(s)</m> to find <m>x(t)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Each term matches <xref ref="formula-B" text="custom">Formula B</xref>,
          <m>\mathcal{L}\{e^{at}\} = \dfrac{1}{s-a}</m>.
          Identify <m>a</m> for each term.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Applying linearity and <xref ref="formula-B" text="custom">Formula B</xref> to each term:
          <md>
            <mrow>x(t) = \mathcal{L}^{-1}\{X(s)\}
              \amp= \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{
                     \underbrace{\frac{\frac{3}{5}}{s-3}}_{\text{Formula B},\, a=3}
                   \right\}
                   + \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{
                     \underbrace{\frac{\frac{7}{5}}{s+2}}_{\text{Formula B},\, a=-2}
                   \right\}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{3}{5}e^{3t} + \frac{7}{5}e^{-2t}.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       POST-EXAMPLE MC CHECKPOINTS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p>And now, a quick check of your understanding of the process. </p>
  <exercise xml:id="ex-laplace-ivp-mc1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Why does taking the Laplace transform of an ODE make it easier to solve?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            It turns the ODE into an algebraic equation in <m>s</m>, which can be solved
            with standard algebra rather than differential equation techniques.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! The derivative formulas replace <m>x'</m> and <m>x''</m> with algebraic
            expressions in <m>X(s)</m>, so the whole equation becomes algebraic.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It eliminates the initial conditions so you only need to find the general solution.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Actually, it is the opposite: the initial conditions are built directly into the
            derivative formulas and used in Step 1.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It converts the ODE into an integral equation, which is easier to evaluate numerically.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The Laplace transform is defined by an integral, but the result is an algebraic
            equation in <m>s</m>, not another integral equation.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It works only for homogeneous equations, where the right-hand side is zero.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The Laplace transform method works for both homogeneous and non-homogeneous equations.
            The right-hand side <m>g(t)</m> just gets transformed along with everything else.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-laplace-ivp-mc2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        At which step in the Laplace transform method are the initial conditions used?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Step 1, when substituting the derivative formulas
            <m>\mathcal{L}\{x'\} = sX(s) - x(0)</m> and
            <m>\mathcal{L}\{x''\} = s^2X(s) - sx(0) - x'(0)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! The values <m>x(0)</m> and <m>x'(0)</m> are plugged in during the
            transform step, so by the time you reach Steps 2 and 3 the initial conditions
            are already incorporated.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Step 2, when solving for <m>X(s)</m> using partial fractions.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            By Step 2 the initial conditions are already baked into the algebraic equation
            from Step 1. Partial fractions just decompose <m>X(s)</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Step 3, when applying the inverse Laplace transform to find <m>x(t)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            By Step 3 the initial conditions have already been used. The inverse transform
            just reads off <m>x(t)</m> from the table.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            They are not used; the Laplace transform method only finds the general solution.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The Laplace transform method finds the particular solution directly, with initial
            conditions built in from the start.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Step 1:</alert> apply <m>\mathcal{L}</m> to both sides, substitute the derivative
          formulas with initial conditions plugged in, and solve for <m>X(s)</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Step 2:</alert> factor the denominator and use partial fractions to decompose <m>X(s)</m>
          into pieces that match the table.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Step 3:</alert> apply <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}</m> to each piece to recover <m>x(t)</m>.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

